Saphira OpenStack documentation
===============================

Saphira Linux — "It's so Simple it Hurts."

OpenStack is famous for being hard to install. That reputation comes
from procedures that assume you already know OpenStack. These guides
invert that: each one takes a bare Saphira machine to a proven role
in one sitting, explains WHY every step exists, and tells you what
to do, what NOT to do, WHEN to do it, and when to leave it alone.

Start here, then read the guide for your role:

  openstack-controller.txt  the control plane (do this first, once)
  openstack-compute.txt     hypervisors (do this per compute host)
  openstack-storage.txt     volume hosts (read before touching disks)
  openstack-pitfalls.txt    every failure from the first bring-up,
                            each with symptom, cause, fix, and why

The fastest path for any role is the setup script in its sibling
package (saphira-openstack-controller, -compute, -storage). The
guides explain what the scripts do and why, so when something
differs on your hardware you know exactly which step to adapt.

These guides assume Saphira packages, Saphira paths (/etc/neutron,
/var/lib/nova, and friends), and curl as the API client. There is
deliberately no python-openstackclient dependency: if you can curl,
you can run a cloud.

Conventions used throughout:
  CTRL            the controller's IP (example lab: 172.16.0.133)
  REGION          region name (example lab: RegionOne)
  physnet1        provider physical network name (your lab may differ)
  br-physnet1     OVS bridge carrying provider traffic
  All passwords in the lab were one test value; yours must differ
  per credential, and must be URL-safe (letters, digits, _ @ -)
  because they are embedded in database URLs.
